==English==
[[Image:Mineral_Mica_moscovita_GDFL107.jpg|thumb|Muscovite]]
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
In 1850 from ''[[Muscovy glass]]'' {{suffix|en||ite}}, from the province Muscovy in [[Russia]]. Named by James Dwight Dana.<ref>Richard V. Gaines, H. Catherine W. Skinner, Eugene E. Foord, Brian Mason, and Abraham Rosenzweig: ''Dana's new mineralogy'', John Wiley & Sons, 1997</ref>

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|mineral}} A pale brown [[mineral]] of the [[mica]] group, being a basic [[potassium]] [[aluminosilicate]] with the [[chemical formula]] [[potassium|K]][[aluminum|Al]]<sub>2</sub>([[silicon|Si]]<sub>3</sub>Al)[[oxygen|O]]<sub>10</sub>(O[[hydrogen|H]],[[fluorine|F]])<sub>2</sub>; used as an [[electrical]] [[insulator]] etc.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James S. Monroe|author2=Reed Wicander|title=The Changing Earth: Exploring Geology and Evolution|edition=5th|location=Belmont, Calif.|publisher=[[w:Cengage Learning|Brooks/Cole]]|year=2009|page=76|isbn=978-0-495-55480-6|passage=Do you enjoy the amber glow seen through the [[isinglass]] window of a wood stove? {{...}} '''''Muscovite''''' (colorless, white, or pale red or green) mica is also common{{nb...}}; it was named for ''Moskva'' (Moscow), where much of Europe's mica was mined. Isinglass, mentioned above, consists of thin, transparent sheets of '''muscovite'''.}}

